I think it could be changed to:

cmpopts.SortSlices(func(p1, p2 criticalPaths) bool {
    return p1.TopologyValue < p2.TopologyValue
}),

I see what you mean, indeed it would be great to get rid of sort() in the Comparer function, or even the entire Comparer function, and instead call LessFn within SortSlices.
I tried implementing that, but it didn't work for me. I see 2 problems:
1) simply
`return p1.TopologyValue < p2.TopologyValue`
would not work, because p1 and p2 are 2-element slices of struct{MatchNum, TopologyValue}. So we need to compare p1[0].TopologyValue vs p2[0].TopologyValue, p1[1].TopologyValue vs p2[1].TopologyValue, and then the MatchNum values too. And somewhere along the way we still need to execute what sort() does:
```
var stateCmpOpts = []cmp.Option{
	cmpopts.SortSlices(func(p1, p2 criticalPaths) bool {
		if p1[0].MatchNum == p2[0].MatchNum {
			if p1[1].MatchNum == p2[1].MatchNum {
				p1.sort()
				p2.sort()
				if p1[0].TopologyValue == p2[0].TopologyValue {
					return p1[1].TopologyValue < p2[1].TopologyValue
				} else {
					return p1[0].TopologyValue < p2[0].TopologyValue
				}
			} else {
				return p1[1].MatchNum < p2[1].MatchNum
			}
		} else {
			return p1[0].MatchNum < p2[0].MatchNum
		}
	}),
}
```

Note that the sort() invocations can be inlined, but that involves code duplication and... well, it gets ugly:
```
if p1[0].TopologyValue > p1[1].TopologyValue {
	// Swap TopologyValue to make them sorted alphabetically.
	p1[0].TopologyValue, p1[1].TopologyValue = p1[1].TopologyValue, p1[0].TopologyValue
}
if p2[0].TopologyValue > p2[1].TopologyValue {
	// Swap TopologyValue to make them sorted alphabetically.
	p2[0].TopologyValue, p2[1].TopologyValue = p2[1].TopologyValue, p2[0].TopologyValue
}
```
So the code gets more complex.

2) Even with SortSlices, we still need a custom comparer... SortSlices would sort the criticalPaths objects, but lessFn does not access the compared objects via pointer - it operates on copies. So if we have a p1 criticalPaths object: `{{TopologyValue: "zone2", MatchNum: 2}, {TopologyValue: "zone1", MatchNum: 2}}`, it will still look the same after lessFn is called. And it will be passed to the default Equals function, which will perform the straightforward object comparison and will assert that objects are not equal:
```
FAIL:     filtering_test.go:2332: PodTopologySpread.RemovePod() returned diff (-want,+got):
          &podtopologyspread.preFilterState{
        -     CriticalPaths:     []*podtopologyspread.criticalPaths{&{{TopologyValue: "zone1", MatchNum: 2}, {TopologyValue: "zone2", MatchNum: 2}}},
        +     CriticalPaths:     []*podtopologyspread.criticalPaths{&{{TopologyValue: "zone2", MatchNum: 2}, {TopologyValue: "zone1", MatchNum: 2}}},
          }
```

So in short, even with a lessFn implemented, Equals still needs the logic in sort() to execute correctly. Which is why in my opinion the current implementation of code is the way to go. I will leave a comment in the code explaining it.
